WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.931
Missing security requirements leave teams without clear expectations for tenant isolation, authentication, authorization, auditability, data retention, rate limits, and administrative workflows.

2
00:00:12.931 --> 00:00:19.706
Unsafe workflows allow attackers to manipulate business logic even when individual endpoints behave as coded.

3
00:00:19.706 --> 00:00:25.822
SaaS products need sequence enforcement, approval checks, abuse prevention, and state validation.

4
00:00:25.822 --> 00:00:30.981
Threat modeling helps teams identify likely abuse paths before code is deployed.

5
00:00:30.981 --> 00:00:37.905
It is most useful when it covers trust boundaries, sensitive data flows, privileged actions, and integrations.

6
00:00:37.905 --> 00:00:47.633
Architectural weaknesses often come from misplaced trust in internal networks, client-side state, third-party callbacks, or service-to-service calls.

7
00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:52.592
SOC 2 readiness benefits from documented design decisions and risk handling.

8
00:00:52.592 --> 00:00:56.424
Use threat modeling for critical workflows and trust boundaries.

9
00:00:56.424 --> 00:01:00.914
Integrate secure SDLC practices into design and development.

10
00:01:00.914 --> 00:01:04.486
Perform security architecture reviews for high-risk features.

11
00:01:04.486 --> 00:01:08.447
Selected SOC 2 mappings for this topic include CC3.

12
00:01:08.447 --> 00:01:11.152
2 - Risk identification; CC8.

13
00:01:11.152 --> 00:01:13.157
1 - Change management processes.
